 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Threshold is -0.40 V min, -0.55 V typ and -0.95 V max at -250 µA, and it falls in magnitude as the junction warms, from about 0.70 V at -55 °C to about 0.36 V at 150 °C (Fig 14). On-resistance is guaranteed at -4.5, -2.5 and -1.8 V of gate drive; as the drive falls below about -3 V it climbs quickly (Fig 12), and at -1.8 V or -2.5 V it rises sharply once the drain current passes about 7 A or 20 A (Fig 13). Taking the gate to -5 V with -10 V and -4.3 A on the drain takes 10 nC typ, 2.6 nC of it Miller charge; the plateau in Fig 6 sits near -1.4 V. The gate is rated ±8.0 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Into a 6.0 Ω drain load from a -6.0 V supply at -1.0 A, driven through 89 Ω, typical times are 11 ns turn-on delay, 32 ns rise, 250 ns turn-off delay and 210 ns fall. Capacitances at -10 V are 830 pF input, 180 pF output and 125 pF reverse transfer, and all three grow as VDS falls (Fig 5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "On a one-inch-square single-layer 1 oz copper FR4 board, junction to ambient is 75 °C/W typ and 100 °C/W max. Power dissipation is rated 1.3 W at 25 °C and 0.8 W at 70 °C ambient, derating at 0.01 W/°C. Continuous drain current is rated -4.3 A at 25 °C and -3.4 A at 70 °C ambient; against case temperature the maximum falls from -4.3 A at 25 °C to zero at 150 °C (Fig 9). On-resistance at -4.5 V rises to about 1.33 times its 25 °C value at 150 °C (Fig 4). Operating junction and storage range is -55 to 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Avalanche",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Single-pulse avalanche energy is rated 33 mJ, from a 25 °C start with 3.5 mH, 25 Ω gate resistance and -4.3 A. The allowed energy shrinks toward zero as the starting junction temperature approaches 150 °C, and is larger at lower current: about 75 mJ at -1.9 A from 25 °C (Fig 10)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The integral diode carries -1.3 A continuous and -34 A pulsed, drops at most 1.2 V at -1.3 A, and recovers from -1.3 A at -100 A/µs in 22 ns typ (33 ns max) with 8.0 nC typ (12 nC max)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package and marking",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The top face carries X Y W LC: X the part code, F for this part; Y the year; W the work week; LC the assembly lot code. A mark above the week code shows lead-free build, and a mark below the year code shows copper wire and halogen-free build. For weeks 1 to 26, Y is the year's last digit and W runs A to Z; for weeks 27 to 52, Y is a letter (A for 2001 or 2011 through K for 2010 or 2020) and W again runs A to Z. Examples: YWW 432 gives DF and 503 gives 5C. The scheme applies to parts built after 02/26/2001."
